# Recognition Heuristic

The recognition heuristic is a mental shortcut where people infer that a recognized object has higher value than an unrecognized one. It is useful for quick decisions under uncertainty but can fail when recognition is based on branding rather than substance.

## Definition

**Recognition Heuristic**: If one of two objects is recognized and the other is not, infer that the recognized object has higher value.

## Example

When choosing between two investment options, you pick the company whose name you recognize, even if you know nothing about its performance. This works reasonably well when recognition correlates with quality (branding).

## Impact

Useful for quick decisions under uncertainty, but can fail when false recognition occurs — such as when branding creates recognition without corresponding quality.

## Limitations

Fails when recognition does not correlate with actual value. Branding and marketing can exploit this heuristic by making mediocre options seem familiar and trustworthy. Fails with false recognition — e.g., choosing a well-known but inferior brand over an unknown superior one.

